    <briefdescription>
    </briefdescription>
    <detaileddescription>
<para>Keeps only the agents currently in use holding language-model KV cache, evicting the rest to reclaim VRAM.</para>
<para><bold>This is a memory-for-latency trade, and it should be opt-in.</bold> With every agent&apos;s contexts resident, switching between agents is nearly free. Evicted, the first message to an agent also pays context allocation and a full prefill — on the order of a second or two — in exchange for a large VRAM saving. That is the right trade on a GPU that would otherwise not fit and the wrong one on a GPU with headroom, which is why bEnabled exists rather than this being unconditional. Drive it from a graphics-quality or &quot;low VRAM&quot; setting.</para>
<para><bold>Why this is in the plugin rather than in your game.</bold> Nothing here is game-specific, and every part of it is a mistake waiting to be made: forgetting the debounce turns scrubbing through a character list into one uncancellable prefill per entry; forgetting the hot set punishes the most common interaction pattern there is; and forgetting that cache operations are idle-only produces intermittent AgentBusy failures that look like server bugs.</para>
<para><bold>Correctness never depends on this.</bold> The server transparently restores an evicted context before the next send, so a badly tuned policy costs time to first token, not behaviour.</para>
<para><bold>Usage.</bold> Add to any Actor, call Register for each agent that should participate (or RegisterAll), and call SetActiveAgent whenever the player&apos;s focus moves. Agents that must always stay warm — a background summariser that runs after every turn, say — should simply not be registered: evicting one guarantees a restore on its next turn, which is strictly worse than never evicting it.</para>
<para>For creation-time behaviour, pair this with <ref refid="class_u_tryll_agent_component_1ae88e6c2a2ab36c2037a1e05c7169f663" kindref="member">UTryllAgentComponent::KvCacheInitialization</ref> = DeferAllocation on the agents it manages. That is where most of the saving comes from: a context costs its full context_size the moment it is created, so never allocating it is worth more than evicting it later. </para>
    </detaileddescription>
